 # Perspective-Correct 3D Gaussian Splatting via Plücker Coordinates
 
-Also called: Hybrid 3D Gaussian Splatting intersection, Inversion-free splat evaluation, Matrix inversion-free ray-splat intersection, Perspective-Correct 3D Gaussian Evaluation, Perspective-Correct Ray-Splat Intersection, Perspective-accurate 3D Gaussian splat evaluation, Plücker coordinate ray-splat intersection
+Also called: Hybrid 3D Gaussian Splatting intersection, Inversion-free splat evaluation, Matrix inversion-free ray-splat intersection, Perspective-Correct 3D Gaussian Splat Evaluation, Perspective-Correct Ray-Splat Intersection, Perspective-accurate 3D Gaussian splat evaluation, Plücker coordinate ray-splat intersection
 
 ## Overview
-This method provides a differentiable, matrix-inversion-free evaluation of 3D Gaussian densities along per-pixel viewing rays, ensuring numerical stability even for degenerate splats where principal axes vanish [S1]. Unlike the affine approximation in standard 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) which causes perspective distortion, this approach achieves fully perspective-correct rendering by extending Weyrich et al.'s 2D splatting techniques to non-degenerate 3D Gaussians using Plücker coordinates [S1]. It eliminates the need for special handling of degenerate cases and avoids the numerical instabilities associated with matrix inversions in alternative rasterization-based approaches [S1].
+This method provides a differentiable, matrix-inversion-free evaluation of 3D Gaussian splats along per-pixel viewing rays to achieve fully perspective-correct rendering [S1]. It solves the perspective distortion artifacts inherent in the affine approximation of standard 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) and the numerical instability of matrix inversions required by alternative ray-tracing or rasterization-based intersection methods [S1]. By extending Weyrich et al.'s bounding box and ray-splat intersection techniques to non-degenerate 3D Gaussians using Plücker coordinates, it ensures numerical stability even for degenerate splats (where principal axes vanish) while maintaining real-time rasterization performance [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs**:
-*   Gaussian primitive: Mean $\boldsymbol{\mu}$, scale $s$, rotation $r$ (defining covariance $\boldsymbol{\Sigma}$).
-*   Camera: Model-view matrix $M$, projection matrix $P$, viewport transform $V$.
-*   Ray: Pixel screen coordinates $(x_s, y_s)$.
-*   Hyperparameter: $\tau_\alpha$ (minimum alpha threshold for bounding box culling) [S1].
+### Inputs and Outputs
+**Inputs:**
+*   Per-splat attributes: Orientation quaternion $(t_u, t_v, t_w)$, scale $(s_u, s_v, s_w)$, center $\mathbf{p}$, covariance $\boldsymbol{\Sigma}$ (or derived rotation/scale matrices), and opacity $\alpha$.
+*   Camera matrices: Model-view matrix $\mathbf{M} \in \mathbb{R}^{4 \times 4}$, projection matrix $\mathbf{P} \in \mathbb{R}^{4 \times 4}$, and viewport transform $\mathbf{V} \in \mathbb{R}^{4 \times 4}$ mapping to window coordinates [S1].
+*   Pixel coordinates: Screen-space coordinates $(x_s, y_s)$ for the current pixel [S1].
+*   Hyperparameter: $\tau_\alpha$, the minimum alpha value for a fragment to be considered (used for cutoff) [S1].
 
-**Outputs**:
-*   Squared Mahalanobis distance $\rho^2$ from the ray to the Gaussian center in normalized space.
-*   Bounding box $[b_1, t_1] \times [b_2, t_2] \times [b_3, t_3]$ in screen space for rasterization culling [S1].
+**Outputs:**
+*   The squared density value $\rho(x)^2$ at the point of maximum contribution along the viewing ray [S1].
+*   A tight 3D axis-aligned bounding box (AABB) in screen space for culling [S1].
 
-**Data Structures**:
-*   **Transformation Matrix $T'$**: A $4 \times 4$ matrix mapping normalized splat space (where the Gaussian is a unit sphere) to screen space. It is constructed from $M, P, V$ and splat attributes (orientation $t_u, t_v, t_w$ and scale $s_u, s_v, s_w$) [S1].
-*   **Plücker Coordinates**: Represented as dual Plücker coordinates $\mathcal{L}_s^*$ derived from the intersection of two planes [S1].
+### Data Structures
