 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity**: The method incurs reduced setup costs per Gaussian compared to methods requiring matrix inversions of the covariance, but slightly higher costs per pixel due to the Plücker coordinate calculations [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity**: Requires storing the transformation matrix and Plücker coordinates per splat/ray evaluation. No additional large matrices are needed [S1].
-*   **Performance**: Achieves real-time frame rates when implemented within a rasterization-based pipeline, comparable to or faster than standard 3DGS depending on the number of pixels covered per primitive [S1]. It is significantly faster than ray-tracing frameworks [MLMP*24] [S1].
-*   **Strengths**:
-    *   Numerical stability: No matrix inversions, handling degenerate splats (vanishing axes) gracefully [S1].
-    *   Perspective Correctness: Eliminates affine approximation artifacts [S1].
-    *   Differentiable: All operations (additions, multiplications, one division) are trivial to differentiate [S1].
-*   **Limitations**:
-    *   Slightly higher per-pixel cost than standard 3DGS affine projection [S1].
-    *   Requires careful handling of the vanishing denominator case (ray missing splat) [S1].
-*   **When to Use**: Ideal for high-quality novel view synthesis where perspective accuracy is critical and degenerate splats may occur during optimization. Particularly beneficial when primitives cover few pixels [S1].
-*   **When Not to Use**: If maximum performance is required and perspective artifacts are acceptable, standard 3DGS affine projection may be faster.
